<body>
<h1>Unit 10: Thermal Expansion</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Heat &amp; Thermodynamics</p><p class="summary">Most solids expand when heated and water does something stranger. The coefficient of expansion is a property with a pedigree - it tracks bond strength - and it is the hidden variable in precision engineering, from railway gaps to cryogenic mirrors.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>For modest temperature changes dL = alpha L dT; alpha is the linear coefficient of expansion and depends on material, temperature and direction.</li><li>Expansion coefficients are broadly inversely related to bond strength, so stiff, high-melting-point materials such as fused silica expand far less than rubber or lead.</li><li>Water is anomalous: it contracts on cooling from 100 degrees C to 4 degrees C and expands on freezing, which is why lakes freeze from the top down and pipes burst in winter.</li><li>A bimetallic strip converts a temperature change into mechanical motion - the working principle of every household thermostat.</li><li>The Gruneisen parameter links expansion to compressibility and heat capacity through alpha V K_s = gamma C_V.</li><li>Gaps in rails, joints in bridges and expansion joints in concrete are all the same idea: the material will change size, so the structure must allow it.</li><li>Thermal expansion comes from anharmonicity in the interatomic potential: the average separation between neighbours grows with temperature, and the expansion coefficient is that average increase per kelvin.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The linear law is a first-order approximation; it fails for large temperature changes, near phase transitions and for anisotropic materials such as graphite or wood.</li><li>For an isotropic solid the volume coefficient is approximately three times the linear coefficient, and the equality holds only in the small-expansion limit.</li><li>Apparent expansion of a liquid must subtract the container's own expansion, which for mercury in glass can be about 2 % of the reading.</li><li>As temperature approaches absolute zero the expansion coefficient vanishes, which is why cryogenic instruments use alloys such as Invar and Kovar whose expansion is nearly zero.</li><li>The linear expansion law is a first-order approximation for small temperature changes; a body cooling towards absolute zero needs the integrated form, because the coefficient itself varies with temperature.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 4 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Linear expansion and its measurement</li><li>Cubical expansion, superficial expansion and their relation with linear expansion</li><li>Liquid expansion: absolute and apparent</li><li>Dulong and Petit method of determining expansivity of liquid</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“All substances expand by the same fraction when heated, so a metal and a glass rod of the same length change length equally.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The coefficient of expansion is a property of the material, set by the anharmonicity of its interatomic potential. It ranges over more than an order of magnitude between common materials.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> alpha is characteristic of the material: about 12 x 10^-6 per K for steel, 23 x 10^-6 for aluminium, and about 0.5 x 10^-6 for fused silica. For a given dT and L, the extension is dL = alpha L dT, so a fused-silica optical element changes length by about 25 times less than a steel one of the same size.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Water expands when it freezes, so the volume expansion coefficient of water is always positive.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Water is anomalous between 0 and 4 degrees C: it contracts on cooling down to 4 degrees C and then expands again as it cools towards freezing, because the open hydrogen-bonded structure of ice occupies more volume.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Water's expansion is negative between 0 and 4 degrees C (density maximum at 4 degrees C) and positive outside that range. Hence the apparent expansion of a liquid in a container, and the freezing of a full bottle into a cracked one, and the reason lakes freeze from the top down.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The cubical expansion coefficient gamma is a completely independent material constant.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> For an isotropic solid the volume and length changes are tied together, so gamma is fixed by alpha. It is not independent data.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> gamma = 3 alpha for a solid (V' = V(1 + 3 alpha dT) to first order), the superficial expansion coefficient is 2 alpha, and for a liquid the cubical coefficient is the sum of the real (cubical) and the apparent (container) contributions.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A 10 m long steel rail (alpha = 12 x 10^-6 per K) is laid with both ends fixed. The temperature rises by 40 K. Find the thermal stress developed in the rail if E = 200 GPa.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Free expansion would be dL = alpha L dT = 12 x 10^-6 x 10 x 40 = 4.8 x 10^-3 m. The ends cannot move, so this expansion is prevented and the rail is compressed by the equivalent strain eps = dL / L = 4.8 x 10^-4. The stress is sigma = E eps = 200 x 10^9 x 4.8 x 10^-4 = 9.6 x 10^7 Pa = 96 MPa. Equivalently sigma = E alpha dT, the standard formula for a constrained bar.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the relation gamma = 3 alpha between the cubical and linear expansivities of an isotropic solid.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Let the solid have side L and volume V = L^3. After a temperature rise dT the side becomes L' = L (1 + alpha dT), so V' = L^3 (1 + alpha dT)^3 = V (1 + 3 alpha dT + 3 alpha^2 dT^2 + alpha^3 dT^3). For small dT the higher-order terms are negligible, so V' = V (1 + 3 alpha dT). Comparing with the definition V' = V (1 + gamma dT) gives gamma = 3 alpha. The same argument for area, A' = A (1 + 2 alpha dT), gives the superficial expansivity 2 alpha.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A telescope mirror is machined from fused silica rather than aluminium. Explain the thermal reasoning, and say what property of the mirror material the design is really protecting.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> A mirror's figure must stay within a fraction of a wavelength of perfect over the night, so its shape must not change as the night temperature swings. An aluminium mirror expands about 23 x 10^-6 per K and fused silica only about 0.5 x 10^-6, so the silica mirror's shape changes roughly 25 times less. The property being protected is really the thermal stability of the figure (and the focal length), not the absolute size: the mirror's expansion is matched by the support and the spacing of the segments, and the design compensates the remaining expansion. The low expansivity of fused silica comes from the very stiff, nearly harmonic Si-O bond, whose low anharmonicity makes the mean interatomic separation almost independent of temperature.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Linear expansion of aluminium</td><td>about 23e-6 per K</td></tr><tr><td>Linear expansion of fused silica</td><td>about 0.5e-6 per K</td></tr><tr><td>Volume expansion of water near 20 degrees C</td><td>about 2.1e-4 per K</td></tr><tr><td>Linear expansion of steel</td><td>about 12e-6 per K</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can thermal expansion be harnessed directly to produce useful electrical power?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Thermal diodes, phase-change engines and flexoelectric effects are active research areas, but the efficiencies remain far below those of conventional heat engines.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does water expand when it freezes, when almost every other substance contracts?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Hydrogen bonds give ice an open lattice; the same anomaly is what lets a lake freeze from the top down.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Design a bimetallic strip switch and calculate the tip displacement for a given temperature change.</li><li>Explain why a mercury thermometer left in a freezer shows a rise in reading rather than a fall.</li><li>Compare the thermal design of the JWST's cryogenic mirror segments with a classroom thermometer and state the common principle.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/thermal-expansion">Read the chapter notes: Thermal Expansion</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-heat-determinations">Heat determinations 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-calc-heat">Heat calculator</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/webbs-mirrors/">NASA: Webb's Mirrors</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-3-thermal-expansion">OpenStax: Thermal Expansion</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
